+# Contract: Recognition Grammar and Precedence (amended)
+
+**Branch**: `002-phone-redaction-out-dir` | **Spec**: [spec.md](../spec.md) | **Research**: [research.md](../research.md)
+
+This **amends** [feature 001's recognition contract](../../001-logredact-cli/contracts/redaction-rules.md)
+by adding one category. The email, IPv4 and payment-card rules in that document are reproduced
+nowhere here and are **unchanged in every respect** — pattern, placeholder, validation, boundary
+rules, and the card rejection/restart rule (FR-005).
+
+Every pattern and every row below was executed against the prototype before this document was
+written: all 27 phone and regression cases from the spec plus 17 further boundary cases produce the
+stated result (research R1, R2).
+
+## Scanning contract *(amended)*
+
+Each line is scanned once, left to right. At each position the first category that matches wins, in
+the order **email → IPv4 → card → phone**. Text consumed by a replacement is never reconsidered. A
+card candidate that fails validation is still **not** a replacement: scanning resumes one character
+after that candidate's start, so text it covered remains eligible.
+
+Expressed as one compiled pattern whose alternation order carries the precedence:
+
+```python
+COMBINED = re.compile(
+    f"(?P<email>{EMAIL})|(?P<ip>{IP})|(?P<card>{CARD})|(?P<phone>{PHONE})"
+)
+```
+
+**`email` before `phone` is load-bearing** and must not be rearranged: both can begin at the same
+`+`, and FR-004 requires email to win. `phone`'s position relative to `ip` and `card` is
+behaviourally inert — a phone candidate begins at `+`, an IPv4 or card candidate at a digit, so they
+can never start at the same offset — and `phone` is appended last to leave 001's documented order
+intact. Verified by running both orders.
+
+A phone candidate has **no rejection path**: unlike a card, everything about it is decided by the
+pattern, so the scan loop gains no new branch.
+
+## International phone number (FR-001, FR-002, FR-003)
+
+```python
+PHONE = r"\+\d{8,15}(?!\d)"
+```
+
+Replacement: `[PHONE]`. No code validation.
+
+- A **literal `+`**, then an **unbroken run of 8 to 15 digits inclusive**. No spaces, hyphens, dots
+  or parentheses inside the run; grouped, national and extension forms are out of scope.
+- The trailing `(?!\d)` is the **over-long rule** (FR-003): a run of 16 or more digits matches
+  nothing at all, rather than having its first 15 digits redacted. Because `\+` requires a literal
+  `+`, no interior offset of such a run can match either, so the run is reproduced whole.
+- There is deliberately **no leading guard**: a `+` preceded by any character still begins a
+  candidate, so `a+12345678` and `tel:+14155552671` are redacted. FR-002 conditions the match on the
+  `+` and the digits that follow it only.
+- Leading zeros are digits like any other: `+012345678` is a phone number.
+
+| Input | Result | Why |
+|-------|--------|-----|
+| `+14155552671` | `[PHONE]` | 11 digits |
+| `+442071838750` | `[PHONE]` | 12 digits |
+| `+12345678` | `[PHONE]` | 8 digits — lower boundary, inclusive |
+| `+123456789012345` | `[PHONE]` | 15 digits — upper boundary, inclusive |
+| `+012345678` | `[PHONE]` | leading zeros are digits |
+| `+1234567` | unchanged | 7 digits, below the range |
+| `+1234567890123456` | unchanged | 16 digits — **no fragment may match** |
+| `+1234567890123456789012` | unchanged | 22 digits, same rule |
+| `14155552671` | unchanged | no `+` |
+| `+ 14155552671` | unchanged | run broken by a space |
+| `+1 415 555 2671` | unchanged | run broken; the parts then fail the card length rule |
+| `call +14155552671.` | `call [PHONE].` | a period is not a digit, so it does not extend the run |
+| `+12345678a` | `[PHONE]a` | a letter does not extend the run |
+| `(+12345678)` | `([PHONE])` | punctuation preserved on both sides |
+| `tel:+14155552671` | `tel:[PHONE]` | no leading guard |
+| `++12345678` | `+[PHONE]` | the second `+` begins the candidate |
+| `+1234567 +12345678` | `+1234567 [PHONE]` | each run judged on its own |
+| a line that is exactly one phone number | exactly `[PHONE]` | terminator preserved |
+
+## Cross-category contract *(new rows)*
+
+The three outcomes below follow from leftmost-match plus the alternation order; no special case
+exists for any of them.
+
+| Input | Result | Category credited | Why |
+|-------|--------|-------------------|-----|
+| `+14155552671@example.com` | `[EMAIL]` | email (1), phone (0) | The email local part already admits `+`, so both candidates start at offset 0 and email is listed first (FR-004) |
+| `+378282246310005` | `[PHONE]` | phone (1), card (0) | 15 digits, Luhn-valid, so a card candidate exists at offset 1 — but the phone candidate starts at offset 0 and leftmost-match selects it. **A deliberate, documented change to today's output**, which was `+[CARD]`. The value is still fully redacted; only the credited category changes |
+| `+4111111111111111` | `+[CARD]` | card (1), phone (0) | 16 digits is outside the phone range, so no phone candidate exists and the card rule applies unchanged |
+| `+1.2.3.4` | `+[IP]` | ip (1) | Only one digit follows the `+`; the IPv4 boundary rule accepts a preceding `+`, as today |
+| `a@b.io,+442071838750,1.2.3.4` | `[EMAIL],[PHONE],[IP]` | 1 / 1 / 0 / 1 | All three replaced, both commas preserved |
+| `4111 1111 1111 1111 +14155552671` | `[CARD] [PHONE]` | card (1), phone (1) | Independent values on one line |
+| `+14155552671,+442071838750` | `[PHONE],[PHONE]` | phone (2) | Every occurrence on a line is redacted (FR-004) |
+
+## Unchanged guarantees (restated for completeness)
+
+| Property | Status |
+|----------|--------|
+| Email, IPv4, card grammars and placeholders | Unchanged (FR-005) |
+| Card rejection restarts one character into the candidate | Unchanged — `123 1.2.3.4` still yields `123 [IP]` |
+| `CARD`'s `(?<!\d)` lookbehind blocks interior offsets | Unchanged — and it is what keeps an over-long `+`-prefixed run whole after a rejected card candidate |
+| Recognition is line-local | Unchanged — a value split across a line ending is not redacted, phone included |
+| Non-UTF-8 bytes | Unchanged — reproduced exactly, never redacted |
+| Determinism | Unchanged |
+| Any input with no `+`-prefixed 8–15 digit run | **Byte-identical to the current tool** (FR-005, SC-006) |
